::Q1::Where can an application securely load an API key instead of hardcoding it? 
{
~A URL parameter visible to users
=An environment variable or dedicated secrets-management service
~A public source-code comment
~A README committed to a public repository
}

::Q2::Why should generated SQL or shell commands not be executed automatically? 
{
~Generated commands are guaranteed to be harmless
~Execution automatically verifies correctness
~Review makes commands invalid
=They may contain incorrect or harmful instructions that have not been checked
}

::Q3::Why is usage monitoring useful for an LLM-integrated system? 
{
=It can help identify unusual usage, errors, or unexpected consumption
~It replaces authentication
~It removes the need for access control
~It guarantees every output is correct
}

::Q4::Why should secrets be excluded from version control? 
{
~AWS requires every secret to be public
~Version control cannot store text
~Secrets make Git repositories unusable
=Committed secrets can be exposed to people or systems that should not have them
}

::Q5::What principle should guide permissions for an LLM-powered feature? 
{
=Least privilege
~Maximum privilege
~Anonymous access
~No authentication
}